WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.141
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.441 --> 00:00:08.619
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.919 --> 00:00:15.994
Unit thirteen: Cultural Criticism. Today you will hold contrasting ideas together with 'even as' and 'even though'.

4
00:00:16.294 --> 00:00:18.865
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.265
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.117 --> 00:00:25.025
Hegemony.

7
00:00:26.017 --> 00:00:30.176
Hegemony means dominance of one group, often through cultural influence.

8
00:00:30.783 --> 00:00:33.642
Hollywood's hegemony shapes global tastes.

9
00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.053
Your turn. Say: Hegemony.

10
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:40.750
Hegemony.

11
00:00:41.517 --> 00:00:42.785
Ideology.

12
00:00:43.783 --> 00:00:47.757
Ideology means a system of ideas and beliefs that shapes behaviour.

13
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.383
Advertising carries a consumerist ideology.

14
00:00:51.983 --> 00:00:54.415
Your turn. Say: Ideology.

15
00:00:57.267 --> 00:00:58.523
Ideology.

16
00:00:59.283 --> 00:01:00.528
Subculture.

17
00:01:01.517 --> 00:01:05.351
Subculture means a group with values distinct from the mainstream.

18
00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:08.217
Skateboarding began as a subculture.

19
00:01:08.817 --> 00:01:11.446
Your turn. Say: Subculture.

20
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:15.487
Subculture.

21
00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:17.437
Resistance.

22
00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:22.272
Resistance means refusal to accept dominant values or power.

23
00:01:22.883 --> 00:01:25.243
Street art can be a form of resistance.

24
00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.560
Your turn. Say: Resistance.

25
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:32.540
Resistance.

26
00:01:33.283 --> 00:01:34.493
Commodification.

27
00:01:35.483 --> 00:01:39.131
Commodification means turning something into a product to be sold.

28
00:01:39.717 --> 00:01:42.575
The commodification of tradition worries elders.

29
00:01:43.183 --> 00:01:46.103
Your turn. Say: Commodification.

30
00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:50.270
Commodification.

31
00:01:51.017 --> 00:01:52.772
Identity politics.

32
00:01:53.783 --> 00:01:58.163
Identity politics means political positions based on group identity.

33
00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:01.911
Identity politics shapes many modern debates.

34
00:02:02.517 --> 00:02:06.260
Your turn. Say: Identity politics.

35
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.088
Identity politics.

36
00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:13.095
Appropriation.

37
00:02:14.083 --> 00:02:18.533
Appropriation means taking elements of another culture without understanding or credit.

38
00:02:19.117 --> 00:02:21.987
The brand was accused of cultural appropriation.

39
00:02:22.583 --> 00:02:25.561
Your turn. Say: Appropriation.

40
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:29.691
Appropriation.

41
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.590
Authenticity.

42
00:02:32.583 --> 00:02:35.442
Authenticity means the quality of being genuine.

43
00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:38.433
Tourists often seek authenticity.

44
00:02:39.017 --> 00:02:41.634
Your turn. Say: Authenticity.

45
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:45.745
Authenticity.

46
00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:50.184
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:57.754
Visal, your essay discusses traditional Khmer patterns appearing on international fashion brands. Is that a good thing?

48
00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:08.236
It's complicated. Even as our culture gains global attention, questions of authenticity and credit remain unresolved.

49
00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:10.723
Where do you draw the line?

50
00:03:11.817 --> 00:03:21.876
Appreciation involves understanding and credit. Appropriation happens when a brand profits from a pattern even though local weavers receive nothing.

51
00:03:22.983 --> 00:03:26.910
And what about young Cambodians who mix traditional and modern styles?

52
00:03:28.017 --> 00:03:35.371
I see that as creative resistance. Even though it changes the tradition, it keeps it alive on our own terms.

53
00:03:36.533 --> 00:03:38.405
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:42.829
Grammar focus: Even as and even though clauses.

55
00:03:43.129 --> 00:03:44.455
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:50.122
Even as culture spreads globally, questions of authenticity remain.

57
00:03:51.017 --> 00:03:54.421
Even though local weavers receive nothing, the brand profits.

58
00:03:55.317 --> 00:03:58.442
Even as the tradition changes, it stays alive.

59
00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:08.649
'Even as' = at the same time as something develops (two simultaneous processes). 'Even though' = despite a known fact. Both are followed by a full clause.

60
00:04:09.383 --> 00:04:14.300
Be careful! Don't say: Even as culture spread globally, questions remains.

61
00:04:16.233 --> 00:04:20.625
Say: Even as culture spreads globally, questions remain.

62
00:04:20.925 --> 00:04:22.947
Check subject–verb agreement.

63
00:04:23.733 --> 00:04:26.246
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:28.371
Who Owns Culture?.

65
00:04:28.671 --> 00:04:34.490
Cultural criticism asks how power operates through everyday objects, images and practices.

66
00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:41.470
Even as globalisation makes cultural exchange easier, it often reproduces existing inequalities.

67
00:04:42.467 --> 00:04:47.357
When a subculture's style is commodified, it may lose the very meaning that made it attractive.

68
00:04:48.367 --> 00:04:55.440
Critics therefore distinguish between appreciation, which involves respect and credit, and appropriation, which does not.

69
00:04:56.433 --> 00:05:01.463
Even though the line can be blurred, asking who benefits is usually a reliable starting point.

70
00:05:02.567 --> 00:05:06.499
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:10.812
blank the city modernises, its old markets are disappearing.

72
00:05:11.112 --> 00:05:14.685
A, Even as. B, Even if. C, Even.

73
00:05:18.233 --> 00:05:20.639
The answer is A: Even as.

74
00:05:21.783 --> 00:05:24.770
Turning a tradition into a product to sell is blank.

75
00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:29.304
A, resistance. B, commodification. C, hegemony.

76
00:05:32.867 --> 00:05:35.482
The answer is B: commodification.

77
00:05:36.617 --> 00:05:39.812
Taking another culture's elements without credit is blank.

78
00:05:40.112 --> 00:05:44.822
A, appropriation. B, authenticity. C, ideology.

79
00:05:48.367 --> 00:05:50.622
The answer is A: appropriation.

80
00:05:51.783 --> 00:05:53.423
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:53.723 --> 00:06:03.704
A, Even though the brand profits, but weavers earn nothing.. B, Even the brand profits, weavers earn nothing.. C, Even though the brand profits, weavers earn nothing..

82
00:06:07.267 --> 00:06:11.600
The answer is C: Even though the brand profits, weavers earn nothing..

83
00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:16.259
According to the reading, what is a reliable starting point?

84
00:06:16.559 --> 00:06:21.629
A, asking who benefits. B, banning exchange. C, ignoring style.

85
00:06:25.167 --> 00:06:27.863
The answer is A: asking who benefits.

86
00:06:29.067 --> 00:06:32.311
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:37.065
Where do you see the line between cultural appreciation and appropriation?

88
00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:55.339
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:55.639 --> 00:07:29.602
For me, the line between appreciation and appropriation depends on respect, understanding and credit. If a foreign designer studies Khmer silk weaving and works with local artisans, that is appreciation. Even though the result is new, the community benefits and is recognised. By contrast, copying a sacred pattern onto cheap products for profit feels like appropriation. Some argue that all culture borrows, which is true. Still, I believe the key question is always who benefits.

90
00:07:29.902 --> 00:07:31.762
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:37.415
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and even as and even though clauses.

92
00:07:37.715 --> 00:07:43.073
Today's value is cultural respect. Learn from other cultures, and give credit to their people.

93
00:07:43.373 --> 00:07:45.932
See you in Unit fourteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:46.232 --> 00:07:49.117
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

